{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.StandardModel.ChiralityGradingConstruction","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ChiralityGradingConstruction","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.List.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Logic.Function.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:QOU.StandardModel.ChiralityIndependentOfCrossing"],"declarations":20,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.flavour_and_particle_even_under_flip","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.flavour_and_particle_even_under_flip","kind":"theorem","line":284,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L284-L293","doc":"**The other two bits are Reeb-blind.**\n\nFlavour and particle-ness are *even* under the chirality involution, so the\ngrading separates exactly the datum it is meant to and disturbs neither of the\nother two labels V9 showed to be independent.  This is what makes the three bits\ncompatible rather than merely distinct. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.gen1_chirality_odd_under_flip","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.gen1_chirality_odd_under_flip","kind":"theorem","line":278,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L278-L282","doc":"And it is odd, as §3 guarantees — recorded directly on the sector so the\nguarantee is visible without unfolding the general theorem. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.gen1_chirality_is_the_constructed_grading","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.gen1_chirality_is_the_constructed_grading","kind":"theorem","line":264,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L264-L276","doc":"**The construction, instantiated, is V9's `chirality`.**\n\n`chirOf gen1Section` is what §3 builds from the left-handed transversal; V9's\n`chirality` is the hand-written bit.  They are the same function on all eight\nstates.\n\nThis is the load-bearing claim of V10.  Without it the construction would be a\nparallel invention that merely *resembles* the corpus's labelling; with it, the\ncorpus's chirality bit **is** the Reeb-orbit grading, for the transversal that\npicks left. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.gen1Section","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.gen1Section","kind":"def","line":258,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L258-L262","doc":"`repL` is a transversal for `flipChirality`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.repL","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.repL","kind":"def","line":251,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L251-L256","doc":"The transversal choosing the **left-handed** member of each chiral pair. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.flipChirality_free","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.flipChirality_free","kind":"theorem","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L245-L249","doc":"It is **free** — no first-generation quark is its own chiral partner.  By\n§2–3 this is exactly the condition for the grading to exist, and here it holds\nby inspection of the eight states. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.flipChirality_involutive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.flipChirality_involutive","kind":"theorem","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L241-L243","doc":"It is an involution. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.flipChirality","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.flipChirality","kind":"def","line":233,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L233-L239","doc":"The involution exchanging the two chiralities, particle and antiparticle\nalike.  This is the `Gen1Quark` shadow of the Reeb involution `ξ ↦ −ξ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.reeb_odd_grading_unique_up_to_flip","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.reeb_odd_grading_unique_up_to_flip","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L218-L229","doc":"**Globally: two odd gradings either agree everywhere or are exact opposites,\norbit by orbit.**\n\nStated as the disjunction at each state, which is the honest form — a grading\nmay agree with another on one orbit and be flipped on the next, since each orbit\ncarries its own independent choice. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.reeb_odd_grading_determined_on_orbit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.reeb_odd_grading_determined_on_orbit","kind":"theorem","line":206,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L206-L216","doc":"**Two odd gradings agreeing at one point of an orbit agree on it.**\n\nAn odd grading has no freedom within an orbit: fixing its value on one member\nfixes the other.  All the choice lives in the transversal, which is why the\nconstruction above is not one option among many but *the* shape of the answer. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.reeb_odd_grading_iff_free","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.reeb_odd_grading_iff_free","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L189-L202","doc":"**The criterion.**\n\nGiven a transversal, a globally Reeb-odd chirality grading exists **exactly\nwhen** the Reeb involution is free.  This is the answer to the question V9 left\nopen, and it names the obstruction precisely: fixed points, i.e. the purely\nhorizontal states, are the only thing that can prevent the grading. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.exists_reeb_odd_grading","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.exists_reeb_odd_grading","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L183-L187","doc":"**Existence, packaged.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.reeb_odd_grading_of_section","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.reeb_odd_grading_of_section","kind":"theorem","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L160-L181","doc":"**The grading built from a transversal is odd.**\n\nThe two cases of `T.mem s` are the two ways the orbit can be oriented, and\nfreeness is what keeps them exclusive:\n\n* `rep s = s` — then `s` is the representative, so `chir s = true`, and `ι s` is\n  not (that would need `ι s = s`, contradicting `hfree`), so `chir (ι s) = false`.\n* `rep s = ι s` — then `ι s` is the representative, so `chir (ι s) = true`, and\n  `s` is not, again by freeness.\n\nEither way `chir (ι s) = ! chir s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.free_of_reeb_odd_grading","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.free_of_reeb_odd_grading","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L142-L156","doc":"**A globally odd grading exists only for a free involution.**\n\nThis is `no_chirality_on_reeb_fixed_state` (V9) stated as a property of `ι`\nrather than of one state: oddness at a fixed point would force\n`chir s = !chir s`.  Physically it is `archimedean-chirality.md`'s \"bosons,\nbeing purely horizontal, are chirality-blind\" — a horizontal (Reeb-fixed) state\nobstructs the grading *everywhere*, not just at itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.chirOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.chirOf","kind":"def","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L134-L138","doc":"The grading built from a transversal: `true` on the chosen representative,\n`false` on its partner. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection.inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection.inv","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L132-L132","doc":"The two members of an orbit choose the same representative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection.mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection.mem","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L130-L130","doc":"The representative lies in the orbit it represents. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection.rep","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection.rep","kind":"def","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L128-L128","doc":"The chosen representative of the orbit through a state. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection.mk","kind":"ctor","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L126-L126","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.OrbitSection","kind":"structure","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ChiralityGradingConstruction.lean#L116-L132","doc":"**A transversal for an involution** — a choice of one representative from\neach orbit.\n\n`mem` says the representative is drawn from the orbit `{s, ι s}` itself, and\n`inv` says the choice does not depend on which member you start from.  Together\nthey are exactly \"pick one element of each orbit\".\n\nThis is the input the construction needs, and it is where choice would enter for\nan infinite state space.  Taking it as a hypothesis keeps\n`chirOf`/`reeb_odd_grading_of_section` choice-free. ","signatureChunk":0}]}